#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa4b13a-c222-5a32-b289-04b8c8285d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "0c2486d6-bb55-52ba-9fd8-6b516d3f7858" :question/math-academy-id "e-5557" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ be vectors such that $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 6$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}+4\\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 25$. If $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$, find $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}$." :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$, we also have that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $4\\mathbf{v}$. So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖u + 4v‖^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + ‖4v‖^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖u + 4v‖^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + (4‖v‖)^{2} \\\\\n‖u + 4v‖^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + 16‖v‖^{2} \\\\\n(25)^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + 16(6)^{2} \\\\\n625 &= ‖u‖^{2} + 576 \\\\\n‖u‖^{2} &= 625 - 576 \\\\\n‖u‖^{2} &= 49\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}=\\Vert\\mathbf{u}\\Vert^2=49\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae3aec8-4d20-5467-9ced-2a9c1bda3afd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minimum Value Using the Triangle Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "112253ae-e0ea-554a-83bf-87f8ba7c9ce7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5558" :question/problem "Given that $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} +3\\mathbf{v} \\Vert=14$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert=2$, find the smallest possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}\\Vert$." :question/worked-solution "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n‖u + 3v‖ \\mid \\le ‖u‖ + ‖3v‖\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}‖u + 3v‖ & \\le ‖u‖ + 3‖v‖ \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖ + 3(2) \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖ + 6 \\\\ 8 & \\le ‖u‖\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert$ is $8$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133507 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2391612a-ee27-5cb2-a194-9b51e6fec1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Pythagorean Theorem and the Cauchy-Schwarz and Triangle Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "5e72235b-a419-5f27-bee7-01be8b49c997" :question/math-academy-id "e-5559" :question/problem "Let the vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ be orthogonal.\nIf $\\mid \\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\mid ^2+5\\Vert\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w}\\Vert^2=29$ and $\\| \\mathbf{u} \\| = \\| \\mathbf{w} \\| = 2$, then what is the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$?" :question/worked-solution "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n29 &= \\mid u \\cdot v \\mid ^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} \\\\\n&\\le ‖u‖^{2} \\cdot ‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} \\cdot ‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} \\\\\n&= 4‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} &= 4‖v‖^{2} + 5(‖v‖^{2} + ‖w‖^{2}) \\\\\n&\\le 4‖v‖^{2} + 5(‖v‖^{2} + 2^{2}) \\\\\n&= 9‖v‖^{2} + 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}29 & \\le 9‖v‖^{2} + 20 \\\\ 9 & \\le 9‖v‖^{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le ‖v‖^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert^2\n$$\n\nimplies that\n\n$$\n1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert\n$$\n\n. Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$ is $1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa4b13a-c222-5a32-b289-04b8c8285d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a52c3b15-7f3a-54cf-b50d-d344d26b8685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{337}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131289} {:answer/id #uuid "e102a30a-58e9-56ca-b603-3fd3d685511b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131290} {:answer/id #uuid "e473836c-095d-522d-9a46-80145fe4328d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131291} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2492ad-f832-557b-a75e-8ae82d578300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131292} {:answer/id #uuid "4be2ca3b-dea8-5f2c-ab94-4eac8e635fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e102a30a-58e9-56ca-b603-3fd3d685511b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131290} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa37048-9b16-5f4d-905e-b7cd97665ef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131288}] :question/id #uuid "78f436e6-da8d-594d-9217-c77afac12ee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-39412" :question/problem "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖v‖ = 7$ and $‖2u + v‖ = 25$. If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖u‖$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2391612a-ee27-5cb2-a194-9b51e6fec1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Pythagorean Theorem and the Cauchy-Schwarz and Triangle Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f8016f-6ef7-57c4-8ffa-937e9afa02d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131343} {:answer/id #uuid "01b1041b-b79e-57d5-95e7-f4b05974c395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.typ